text:
I don't see anybody trying to defend C++ (probably someone did this, but I couldn't find it), so here I go. I stopped watching the video halfway, at the end of this comment you will understand why.
0:05 - I would say that the number of initializations is a bit inflated here. For example, "int x=int(10)" does two things: converts 10 to int (which does nothing, since 10 is already an int) and initialize x as 10. You can even argue that is three things: convert 10 to int, create a variable x and write 10 to x. You can call it another way of initialisation, but you can also say that you can initialize with "int x=10;" and "int x=10+0-0/69;", creating an inifinite number of initializations. Also writing "auto x=(1,0);" is pure evil, I don't think anybody does that (unless they want to troll readers of the code). The return value of comma operator is probably a bad design decision, but it's not initialization issue. 
0:33 - I don't get the hype around std::print fuction. The printf function was here for decades and print doesn't look like a big upgrade. Maybe I'm wrong, maybe it works better for printing classes or something. But it looks like some C++ programmers deliberately refuse to use functions that are from C.
0:53 - Random numbers - fully agree. Again, in C++ you can use rand() (which is from C), but mt19937 is "more random".
1:50 - "int i = (int) myFloat;" works in C++ as well, you can use static_cast to have more safety in some scenarios (usually pointers), but you don't have to.
2:32 - Instead of "MyClass* obj=new MyClass();" you can simply use "MyClass obj();" and in 80%-95% of cases (depending on your style of writing code) this works as well. 
3:00 - I agree that working with multiple files in C++ is hard.
5:15 - I agree that interfaces in Java are better than in C++. But in Java, it is much harder to avoid them. Let's say that there are 2 classes: Triangle and Rectangle. I want a function that calculates total area in a list of triangles or rectangles. If both Triangle and Rectangle have a area() function, then in C++ all you need to do is to write something like this:
template<class T>
double totalArea(vector<T>& shapes){
  double sum=0;
  for(auto shape:shapes){
    sum+=shape.area();
  }
  return sum;
}
while in Java you need to write interface for Shape and explicitly write that Triangle and Rectangle implements Shape to do a similar thing. Alternatively, you can do some ugly type checking inside the totalArea function.
10:00 - vector is a tuple of n numbers, where n is a dimension of a space. The only problem is that vectors can be added or multiplied by a number and C++ doesn't support that (i.e. you can't write 5*x to get a vector where every number is 5 times bigger). You can think of a vector as an object with magnitude and direction, but when you want to write it down, you usually use its coordinates. So after all, vector is not a terrible name choice.
10:20 - "Set and map are not what you think they are" - I mean, why should I think that set is hashset? In Java, Set is an interface, so it may be either HashSet or TreeSet. In Python, set is a hashset, but I think the main reason is that in Python standard library there is no treeset. Many languages has their naming conventions, and blaming C++ has its own make no sense, especially that C++ is older most of the other languages shown for comparison. 
26:51 - The problem with modern C++ is that many people thinks that newer is better. Let's imagine that we use this idea in mathematics. Then we shouldn't use Pythagorean theorem, since law of cosines is newer and more general. We shouldn't use Riemann integral, since Lebesgue integral is more general. Actually, we shouldn't use Lebesgue integral either, since Bochner integral is even more general. The problem with this approach is that newer things are better in the sense that they are more general, but they usually are also harder to learn and harder to use. I'm pretty sure the "C-style C++" name comes from a usual C++ programmer who rarely always use the "modernerest" approach as was tired of hearing that his code is outdated.
29:50 - "If you like C++, then you don't know it well enough" - aah, the ancient reverse skill issue. xD
33:46 - I've realized that I never had a problem with returning a pointer to a locally created object because whenever I want to return an object big enough that using a pointer would be a good idea, I use out-parameter technique (i.e. one of the parameters is used as output).
34:01 - I don't think that const has anything to do with speed. It can be used to increase code safety (in similar way to making a class member private). Some people (including me) thinks that it's usually not worth a hassle, since the safety benefit is marginal and putting const in one place often forces you to put it in another.
37:57 - "Many flags have cryptic names that aren't self-explanatory" - Once I had a problem with Java, and I found that the solution is to add -XX:-OmitStackTraceInFastThrow flag. What does the flag do? It ensures that stacktraces are NOT omitted in some cases. Maybe this flag name wasn't cryptic, but is very misleading and I think that is even worse.
39:23 - Fully agree, CMake is scary.
40:35 - Again, fully agree, using anything else than a standard library is usually a nightmare.
49:01 - "The debugger often takes you deep into the internals of the standard library instead of pointing to a clear failure in your own source code" - I mean, that's the idea why whenever an error occurs, a stacktrace is shown. If you know that some code is correct, you jump out on the stacktrace until you reach your own code.
52:08 - Implicit suggestion that vector.end and vector.back should be switched is a terrible idea. First of all, in English back sometimes means last, like the back page of a newspaper means the last page, while endpapers of a book is used to protect the actual content of the book. So the natural order is begin, front, middle/inside/main, back, end. Also, if you would like to switch back with end, then for consistency you should also switch begin and front.
53:00 - "vector<bool> is slower because bools are packed together." Is it, really? Because I've never noticed a significant difference in speed between vector<char> and vector<bool>. But maybe it's because it was never a critical part of my code.
54:46 - using if(map[key]) in C++ as existence check only makes sense if you don't use 0 as values (and value can be converted to bool). Also, if(map[key]) doesn't work either in Python (where using a nonexistent key gives you a KeyError) or Java (where it simply wouldn't compile). I think the only reason if(map[key]) was given as an example was that it kinda does what the author wanted, but not quite. 
55:00 - "The ergonomics of hashmaps in Java and Python are way better"
I would argue the opposite. If I use hashmap as a counter, than all I have to do is 
map[key]++;
In Python I have to do 
if key in map:
  map[key]+=1
else:
  map[key]=1
while in Java
if(map.containsKey(key))
  map.put(key,map.get(key)+1);
else
  map.put(key,1);
And yes, in Python there is a Counter class, but I like the C++'s simplicity. 
55:09 - instead of if(map.find(key)!=map.end()) you can use if(map.count(key))
55:22 - let's be honest, please tell me WTF is this code if not a pure ragebait.
if(map.find(1)!=map.end()){
  map[1]=1000;
} else{
  map[1]=1000;
}

While you show JavaScript as an example of benchmarking. JavaScript has its own problems as well as not being considered as systems language.

