EDIT: Yep, immediately after that, we get into the "C++ sucks in ways that using legacy C syntax doesn't help."

I'm sure we'll get to more "actually the way this must be done in C++" stuff later, but two minutes in and so far I've seen three "the 'C++ way' is dumb, but you can just do it the C way and it makes sense" things.

Random: <include cstdlib> <include ctime> srand(time(0)); rand()%range;
Print: <include cstdio> double e = 2.718281828; printf("e is %lf\n", e);
cast: (int)character;

All things you can do in C++. They're not the "right" way, but you can do them, and the majority of the time there's not any disadvantage to doing things the C way in C++. (For these cases, anyway. There are definitely occasions when the C way is absolutely the unsafe way.) A case could be made for C casts being unsafe, but considering how careful you should be about casting in statically-typed languages in the first place, the main advantage of C++ casts is just that you can search "static_cast" and "dynamic_cast" more easily than (type) casts.

