1:49:31: Counter example on why instant destruction would be bad. std::lock locks the mutex in the constructor and unlocks it in the destructor. With last-use destruction the mutex would be locked and instantly unlocked, causing whatever code is supposed to run with the mutex locked to be run with the unlocked mutex. This would break nearly every major C++ codebase.

void SomeFunc()
{
    std::lock _(SomeMutex);

    // Operations that should only happend while we hold the mutex
}

@1:05:14 You don't need to create new data types and functions for every collection type you make in C. You just need to use macros.

For example, suppose you want a function that adds a value to an array.

#define array_add(_array, _value) do { \
    if ((_array)->length == (_array)->capacity) \
        break; \
    (_array)->value[(_array)->length] = _value; \
    (_array)->length++; \
} while (0)

That's it. The thing is, you don't have an 'array_t' type. You just *make* one when you need it. E.g.

#define array_new_from_buffer(_buffer) { \
    .capacity = sizeof(_buffer) / sizeof(*(_buffer)), \
    .value = (_buffer) \
}

float valuebuf[10];

struct {
    int capacity, length;
    float *value;
} myarray = array_new_from_buffer(valuebuf);

array_add(&myarray, 3.5);
array_add(&myarray, 6.7);

The macros don't care what types you're using or what other data you have in your struct. This is one of those extremely powerful idioms that makes implementing generic collections in C so much less of a pain in the ass.

#ifdef _DEBUG
// bounds check,etc
#endif

