Dumbest take ever. Your comparisons are ridiculous. You're mostly comparing extremely high-level, interpreted languages to something that extremely low-level and compiled. These comparisons are computationally invalid/ C++ is verbose because it doesn't have the same abstractions that the other languages have, because an abstracted language is a high-level one.

1) The different variable initialisations exist for different, specific reasons. I won't say that C++ syntax isn't messy sometimes, because it is, but these exist for a reason and increase the clarity of low-level code. C++ is capable of things that Python and JavaScript aren't, because they're not true programming languages and C++ is. A fairer comparison would have been Rust (which has its own issues). Even Java isn't a proper comparison because it's also a high-level language with a pure OOP paradigm, making it incomparable.

2) Dumb. "Can we finally get a normal print function..." My brother in Christ, C-style printf() and sprintf() exist, along with puts() and putchar(). Like most things, there are multiple ways and scenarios for use-case. Again, it's a low-level that's capable of running on bare-metal; there HAS to be multiple ways with multiple use-cases otherwise it's not a truly low-level language that fits every niche it needs to. Your example code was also utterly idiotic. Nobody writes std::cout the way you showed - that was a sensationalist example designed to resonate with people that don't know any better. You would do something more like:
std::cout << "First Name: " << firstName << "\n";
std::cout << "Last Name: " << lastName << "\n";
etc.

Also, if you're talking about print statements as if they're the be-all and end-all in programming, you're still stuck on a beginner/intermediate level. Print statements are used exclusive for *!simple!* debugging in production, real-world code. They're not super relevant outside of that. 

3) Yes, rand is more complex in C++ than Python, because Python is, at its core, an abstraction layer on-top of C. This shows just how little you actually know about computing: Generating random numbers is not actually as computationally simple as it seems and there's a wealth of articles, books, etc. written around the concept. These articles and books use C or C++ just as an example language to demonstrate a fundamental facet of computing; this isn't a language issue, it's an issue with computers. Also, std::random_device is the *!LEAST!* common way of generating random numbers (in 10 years of C++, I've literally NEVER used it), so you don't "HAVE" to do that - here's a fully functional program to generate and print a random number between 0 - 100 and is compliant in both older ISO C++ standards, and modernd ISO standards:

<code>
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>

int main() {
    srand(time(NULL)); //Seed random gen with time, so it's closer to true-rand (no such really exists with computer)
    int random_int = rand() % 101; // generate rand between 0 and 100
    std::cout << "Random number: " << random_int;

    return 0;
}
</code>

yes, it's more verbose than high level languages, because you have a much more granular control over the rand generation. The fact that you don't understand this or why it's useful and important shows the level at which you're operating and invalidates opinions you have on low-level languages.

4) Again, stupid. You have a VERY low-level language so these bench marks are A) more complex because of the level at which you're operating and B) offer a more nuanced, granular control.

5) Yet again, you fail to understand the different between high-level and low-level languages. C-style casting is possible in C++: char[2] hi = "Hi" -- int cast = (int) hi;. This was changed from C++ to see to fix safety issues and increase clarity. Java (your high-level and therefore not comparable example) does it literally the same way as C++, it just happens under-the-hood; this is abstraction and again, I'm going to keep harping on about this because it invalidates everything you're saying, C++ is a LOW LEVEL LANGUAGE!

6) I actually agree and think unique pointers are dumb. I don't use them and still don't have memory leaks. Low Level Game Dev has a great video on this about writing code without memory leaks.

7) Huh? What are you on about?? What a bizarre statement. Global variables are extremely easy to create lmao. I've literally never heard anyone complain about this - first way (global for file-scope):

<code>
#include <iostream>

int test = 69; // Global, can be used by any func. in this source file

void MyFunction() {
    std::cout << "MyFunction(): "<< test;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << test << "\n";

    MyFunction();
    return 0;
}
</code>

Project scope: The easiest way to do this is to simply put the var in a header that is then included in all source files that need it. This A) avoids the issues of unnecessary global namespace pollution and B) means it's still only included where it's needed, but technically accessible to all.

There, it was that simple. People are saying how knowledgeable you are, in the comments, but to anyone who ACTUALLY knows the language, you're coming across as very inexperienced.

8) While I agree that this is definitely confusing in the beginning, it's necessary to provide both safety AND low-level control. You cannot have a low-level language that engages scope-safety, while also NOT having this. It would need some form of interpretation by a compile-time or run-time interpreter. In the former case, the compiler could not be trusted to do this accurately and, in the latter case, it's no longer a low-level language and has morphed into Java.

8b) "...repurposes existing keywords..." with regards to "static" and "inline": that's actually a hard-agree from me. I don't like that either and is a valid point overall.

9) int types: Once again you've skipped the fact that this isn't a language issue, it's an issue with how computers work on a fundamental, low level. You are specifying the amount of space that you want the variable to be able to occupt to give proper low-level control over memory. If a low-level language doesn't have this, it's an issue. You have to specify int width in Assembly too, when working with memory. It's just low-level computing.

9b) _t - this is entirely opinionated and some people prefer this explicit syntax because it's clearer. Personally not a huge fan either, but it's strangely nitpicky of you.

9c) It's so obvious that you don't understand how computers actually work under the hood. These exist to allow you to control memory usage and optimise your program so that it doesn't use unnecessary memory, which is why well-written C++ apps out perform apps in most other languages - Rust is an example that also does this and is even MORE explicit than C++.

10) C++ devs not agreeing on formatting: Yeah, most of this is just a split between older programmers coming from C and Assembly languages, arguing with the modern world; in one form or another, this is a tale as old as time. The rule of thumb is just do what your employer says. As for the non-C based stuff: Not really an issue, just different opinions on what is or is not more readable, because different brains see text (even if it's code-text) differently and therefore perceive it differently. No one opinion is more valid than another on this. I don't agree with a lot of Java's 'rules', because I'm dyslexic and struggle with the verbosity; it doesn't make it clearer for me, it makes it harder to understand specifically because of the dyslexia. Does this make Java's agreed formatting wrong? No. Does it make me wrong? No. Just different humans having a different experience.

10b) The industry standard for tabs vs spaces is spaces. You just bind 4 (usually) spaces to your tab key, because it's a different binary for the tab character than space characters, and space chars are easier to work with. The really irritating argument is whether it's 2 spaces, 3 spaces, or 4.

11) Names in the STD - Yeah, no I agree. Fully - the naming is definitely a mess. While obviously an opinion, I think most people would agree that naming something different than it actually is, is dumb; you wouldn't name your son "Daughter" and your daughter "Son" lol. I don't know who's running the current ISO committee on C++, but they're clearly smoking something incredible.

12) I get your point here and agree it's irritating but, strictly speaking, other languages function like this under-the-hood and it's abstracted from you which, while nice, once again comes back to low vs. high level.

I'm going to stop here - I've written enough and wasted enough time on this. It's a ridiculous video born of the opinions of someone who lacks a fundamental understanding of how computers work and, therefore, why C and C++ work this way.

