C++ is not hard imo. Basically, 
you have a main function where all your code is run
int main() {
   // code
   return 0;
}
You can declare a variable like 
type var = value
There are integers, which are whole numbers, floats, which are decimals, and other stuff that doesn't really matter. 
An example of a variable would be 
int main() {
   int var = 0; 
   return 0; 
}
You can declare a function like 
return_type func(type arg1, type arg2...) {
}
An example would be
int add(int a, int b) {
   return a + b; 
}
int main() {
   int c = add(1, 3); 
   return 0; 
}
If statements run the code inside the curly braces if the operation returns true 
if(i == 5) {
     // code
}
For loops run through the code until the condition returns true, incrementing the variable by the given amount
for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
   // code
}
while loops run the code until the condition returns true
while(open()) {
   // code
}
That's basically the basics 
Oh, and there's the standard library, which is included with #include <iostream> 
#include <iostream>
