I am working on some Qt projects in my current company, and the unofficial style guide is : 
No spaces between parameters or operators, for example  I see : 
void someFunction(int param1,int param2,QString word)  // last parameter should better be : const QString& word)
{
    int result=param1+param2;
   //..... more code that does not modify word ... and that is why the parameter should be const QString& word 
  if (word==something)
    //more code...
}
If the function was lets say a getter function, there was no const ! 
int getAge()    // should be : int getAge() const 
{ return age; }  

Also : no comments were written. Bad names for most of the methods and the parameters and the private members of the classes. 
Methods that were only helper methods (i.e. used only by the class itself and not by any other class) were left as public  methods, instead of turning them into private methods. 
No range-based for loops are ever used, even though the project is C++14
The constructors do not use initializer-list.  Just assignments inside the body. 
Private members that can have a simple default value during construction, were not assigned that value in the definition (header file) of the class, during the declaration of the member, but were getting the value in the constructor body.  So instead of 
someClass {
  int member1{0};
  bool flag=false;
//....
public:
  someClass();
//....
};

it is like so :
someClass {
  int member1;
  bool flag;
//....
public:
  someClass();
//....
};
//.cpp file : 
someClass::someClass() { 
  member=1; 
  flag=false;
}

